
In Python, instance variables are tied to a specific object and can differ across instances, 
while class variables are shared across all instances of the class and maintain a common value unless explicitly overridden.

Key Differences Between Instance and Class Variables

Feature                     	Instance Variables	                  Class Variables
Definition	Declared inside methods (usually __init__) using self.	Declared directly inside the class but outside any method.
Scope	      Belong to a single object (instance).	                  Belong to the class itself and shared across all instances.
Storage	    Each object has its own copy.	                          Only one copy exists, shared by all objects.
Usage	      Used for attributes unique to each object.            	Used for attributes common to all objects.
Access	    Accessed via self.variable_name.	                      Accessed via ClassName.variable_name or self.variable_name.
Modify    	Changing one instance’s variable not affect others.   	Changing via the class affects all instances.


 Example in Python

class Dog:
    # Class variable
    species = "Canis familiaris"

    def __init__(self, name, age):
        # Instance variables
        self.name = name
        self.age = age

# Create instances
dog1 = Dog("Buddy", 3)
dog2 = Dog("Lucy", 5)

# Accessing instance variables
print(dog1.name)   # Buddy
print(dog2.name)   # Lucy

# Accessing class variable
print(dog1.species)  # Canis familiaris
print(dog2.species)  # Canis familiaris

# Modifying instance variable
dog1.age = 4
print(dog1.age)  # 4
print(dog2.age)  # 5

# Modifying class variable
Dog.species = "Canis lupus"
print(dog1.species)  # Canis lupus
print(dog2.species)  # Canis lupus

1. What They Are
•	Instance Variables
o	Defined inside methods (commonly __init__) using self.
o	Each object (instance) gets its own copy.
o	Used for attributes that differ per object.
•	Class Variables
o	Defined directly inside the class body, outside methods.
o	Shared across all instances of the class.
o	Used for attributes that should be common to all objects.

2. Storage & Memory
•	Instance variables are stored in the object’s __dict__.
Example:
•	dog1 = Dog("Buddy", 3)
•	print(dog1.__dict__)  # {'name': 'Buddy', 'age': 3}
•	Class variables are stored in the class’s __dict__.
Example:
•	print(Dog.__dict__['species'])  # 'Canis familiaris'


3. Access & Resolution Order
Python follows a lookup chain when you access an attribute:
1.	Check the instance’s namespace (obj.__dict__).
2.	If not found, check the class’s namespace (Class.__dict__).
3.	If not found, check parent classes (Method Resolution Order, MRO).
👉 This means if an instance variable has the same name as a class variable, the instance variable overrides it for that object.
Example:
class Dog:
    species = "Canis familiaris"  # class variable

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name  # instance variable

dog1 = Dog("Buddy")
dog2 = Dog("Lucy")

dog1.species = "Canis lupus"  # creates an instance variable
print(dog1.species)  # Canis lupus (instance variable overrides)
print(dog2.species)  # Canis familiaris (still class variable)
print(Dog.species)   # Canis familiaris (class unchanged)

4. Modification Behavior
•	Modifying instance variables affects only that object.
•	Modifying class variables via the class affects all instances that don’t override it.
•	Modifying class variables via an instance creates a new instance variable instead of changing the class variable.

5. Practical Use Cases
•	Instance variables:
o	Object-specific data (e.g., name, age, balance).
o	Example: Each Car object has its own color and mileage.
•	Class variables:
o	Shared constants (e.g., species, company_name).
o	Counters or caches (e.g., tracking how many objects have been created).
Example with a counter:
class Employee:
    company = "TechCorp"   # class variable
    count = 0              # class variable

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name   # instance variable
        Employee.count += 1

e1 = Employee("Alice")
e2 = Employee("Bob")

print(Employee.count)  # 2
print(e1.company)      # TechCorp
print(e2.company)      # TechCorp

6. Best Practices
•	Use instance variables for data unique to each object.
•	Use class variables for data shared across all objects.
•	Avoid naming collisions between instance and class variables unless intentional.
•	Access class variables via the class name (ClassName.var) for clarity.



